- graph
    Worker (Agent)
        is bottleneck
        is tangible: no
        useful life: infinite
        write off method: not applicable
        worker pool: 1
    Optimisation
        worker
        - volume
            - concurrent (single-threading)
                how: one worker interleaves tasks to remove bottleneck
                cost: overhead of switching between tasks
                seen in: node.js event loop
                is bottleneck: yes
                - cheaper resource
                    how: too few resources, so make them cheap enough to spare
                    cost: some of what is paid for goes unused
                    seen in: commodity server clusters
                    is bottleneck: yes
                    - diversified copies (multi-version concurrency)
                        how: each reader gets its own version, readers don't block writers, writes are validated only on commit, no locks
                        cost: storage for old versions
                        seen in: sql server in-memory oltp
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - buffer ("slack")
                        how: hold spare capacity between steps
                        cost: idle capacity
                        seen in: safety stock
                        is bottleneck: yes
                        - held by me
                            how: keep the buffer in my own process
                            cost: lost if my process dies
                            seen in: in-process queue
                            is bottleneck: yes
                        - another party
                            how: let a separate system hold the buffer
                            cost: one more system to run and a hop to reach it
                            seen in: message broker
                            is bottleneck: yes
                    - fastest gun redundancy
                        how: fire at several replicas and take first answer
                        cost: extra load from duplicate requests, multiple nodes with same data
                        seen in: hedged requests
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - optimistic concurrency
                        how: speculate without a lock then reconcile on rare miss
                        cost: redone work on each miss
                        seen in: compare-and-swap
                        is bottleneck: yes
                - no shared state
                    how: shared state, so remove it or pay less for it
                    cost: design effort to keep parties apart
                    seen in: shared-nothing architecture
                    is bottleneck: yes
                    - delete (design away)
                        how: redesign until nothing is shared
                        cost: redesign effort
                        seen in: stateless services
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - clamp into single complex (no shared medium)
                        how: fuse the parties into one unit
                        cost: parts can no longer change alone
                        seen in: enzyme complexes
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - coordinate once, offline, by convention
                        how: agree the rule up front, no talking at run time
                        cost: run-time flexibility
                        seen in: driving on the left
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - pass messages and isolate (guard)
                        how: one owner holds the state, others ask it
                        cost: a message hop per access
                        seen in: erlang actors
                        is bottleneck: yes
                        - separate service
                            how: put the state in a service only it can touch
                            cost: network calls and partial failure
                            seen in: microservice owning its data
                            is bottleneck: yes
                        - hide information
                            how: keep the state private behind an interface
                            cost: every new need goes through the interface
                            seen in: private fields
                            is bottleneck: yes
                    - reduce costs
                        how: keep the sharing, pay less for it
                        cost: weaker guarantees
                        seen in: eventually consistent stores
                        is bottleneck: yes
                        - free riding
                            how: reuse coordination another party already paid for
                            cost: no control over when it happens
                            seen in: request coalescing
                            is bottleneck: yes
                        - unreliable channels
                            how: stop guaranteeing delivery
                            cost: consistency or availability
                            seen in: dns
                            is bottleneck: yes
                - interchangeable resource
                    how: need tied to one resource, so let any fitting resource serve it
                    cost: resources must be kept equivalent
                    seen in: fungible commodities
                    is bottleneck: yes
                    - de-correlate the need from the specific resource
                        how: ask for any resource that fits, not a named one
                        cost: no say in which resource you get
                        seen in: load balancer
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - substitute collateral
                        how: pledge a different asset of equal value
                        cost: checking the swap is worth the same
                        seen in: tri-party repo
                        is bottleneck: yes
                - satisficing (good enough optimisation)
                    how: requirement stricter than needed, so accept good enough
                    cost: the stricter outcome is given up
                    seen in: approximate query processing
                    is bottleneck: yes
                    - net and clear
                        how: offset what parties owe each other, settle only the difference
                        cost: a central party everyone must trust
                        seen in: clearing house
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - collapse the obligation graph
                        how: cancel closed loops of obligations
                        cost: every party must join in
                        seen in: portfolio compression
                        is bottleneck: yes
                - outsourced dependency to whoever can handle it better
                    how: dependency held by the wrong party, so hand it over and pay for it
                    cost: control
                    seen in: managed database
                    is bottleneck: yes
                - synchronise
                    how: tasks wait for each other
                    cost: time spent waiting
                    seen in: python threading
                    is bottleneck: yes
                    - re-entrant lock
                        how: wait for the holder to release, unless it is you
                        cost: must release once per acquire
                        seen in: java synchronized blocks
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - event
                        how: wait for a signal from another task
                        cost: a missed signal leaves the waiter hanging
                        seen in: windows event objects
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - condition
                        how: wait for shared state to change
                        cost: must recheck the state on every wake
                        seen in: producer-consumer queue
                        is bottleneck: yes
                    - barrier
                        how: wait for every task to arrive
                        cost: everyone waits for the slowest task
                        seen in: map and reduce phases
                        is bottleneck: yes
            - externalised work
                how: work on the bottleneck, so move it off
                cost: handing work over and collecting the result
                seen in: background jobs
                is bottleneck: no
                - pre-computed
                    how: do the work before it is needed
                    cost: storage, stale answers
                    seen in: materialised views
                    is bottleneck: no
                - asynchronised
                    how: do the work with external instrument while the worker moves on, a wrapped co routine suspends until its awaited event
                    cost: straight-line control flow
                    seen in: python asyncio
                    is bottleneck: no
                - parallel
                    how: do the work alongside on another worker, pool size not known up front, threads share memory and processes don't
                    cost: exposure to race conditions and deadlocks
                    seen in: web server worker pool
                    is bottleneck: no
                    worker pool: many
                    - queue
                        how: workers take the next task from one shared queue
                        cost: lock contention on the shared queue
                        seen in: thread pool executor
                        is bottleneck: no
                        worker pool: many
                    - dedicated
                        how: each worker owns its share of the work, no lock
                        cost: one worker idles while another is swamped
                        seen in: kafka partition consumers
                        is bottleneck: no
                        worker pool: many
                    - semaphore
                        how: take a permit to run, permit count caps the workers
                        cost: tasks wait for a free permit
                        seen in: connection pool limit
                        is bottleneck: no
                        worker pool: permit count
                - defer
                    how: do the work later, once the rush is over
                    cost: late results
                    seen in: write-behind cache
                    is bottleneck: no
        - variation
            - reduce issue sensitivity
                - aggressive risk detection
                - lower risk appetite
                - dissimilarity in design and manufacturing
                - supplier management
            - constrain design
                - increase expected stress tolerance
                - increase redundency (alternative success path)
                - imporve testability of system and components
            - control availabiltiy
                - preventative and predictive maintenance
                - reduce impact severity
                - reduce time to repair
        - value-add
    Time (Timing)
        - Milestone
            precision
            interval count: 0
            format
                - Time Only
                    seconds precision: no seconds
                    date precision: no date
                - Date
                    precision: no time
                - Date Time
                    precision: no seconds
                - Precise Date Time
                    precision: microseconds
        - Duration
            interval count: 1
        - Cycle
            interval count: 2 or more
        result
        is done
        exception
        start condition (after)
        interval: 5 seconds
        interval count
        timeout
        polling duration
        precision: up to minutes
        displacement from greenwich meridian hours: +0
    Causation
        ...
    
